body.proof_tex:
Fix a known \(x_0\in[0,1]^d\).  The constants below may depend on the fixed tuple \((d,\alpha,\beta,\gamma,L)\), but not on \(n\) or \(x_0\).

For the upper bound, use the total finite Borel estimator \(\widehat\tau_n(x_0)\) supplied by \(\mathrm{lem:closed\mbox{-}regime\mbox{-}fixed\mbox{-}point\mbox{-}upper}\).  Under \(\mathrm{ass:below\mbox{-}elbow}\), that lemma gives
\[
 \sup_{P\in\mathcal P(d,\alpha,\beta,\gamma,L)}
 \mathbb E_{P_O^{\otimes n}}
 \left|\widehat\tau_n(x_0)-\tau_P(x_0)\right|
 \leq C\rho_n .                                                   \tag{1}
\]
The infimum in \(\mathrm{def:minimax\mbox{-}risk}\) ranges over every Borel data-only estimator and therefore includes this estimator.  Consequently,
\[
 R_n
 \leq
 \sup_{P\in\mathcal P(d,\alpha,\beta,\gamma,L)}
 \mathbb E_{P_O^{\otimes n}}
 \left|\widehat\tau_n(x_0)-\tau_P(x_0)\right|
 \leq C\rho_n .                                                   \tag{2}
\]

For the converse, invoke \(\mathrm{lem:closed\mbox{-}regime\mbox{-}fixed\mbox{-}point\mbox{-}component\mbox{-}mixtures}\).  It supplies finite priors \(\Pi_+\) and \(\Pi_-\), both supported on the same class \(\mathcal P(d,\alpha,\beta,\gamma,L)\), such that \(\tau_P(x_0)=\theta_\varepsilon\) is constant for \(P\) in the support of \(\Pi_\varepsilon\),
\[
 \Delta:=|\theta_+-\theta_-|\geq c_0\rho_n,                       \tag{3}
\]
and the genuine fixed-\(n\) observed-data mixtures
\[
 \mathsf M_\varepsilon
 :=\int P_O^{\otimes n}\,d\Pi_\varepsilon(P),
 \qquad \varepsilon\in\{+,-\},                                  \tag{4}
\]
satisfy
\[
 H^2(\mathsf M_+,\mathsf M_-)\leq\frac14.                         \tag{5}
\]
Thus no conditioning or Poissonization enters the decision-theoretic experiment in (4).

For completeness, we derive the testing reduction.  Let \(\nu=\mathsf M_++\mathsf M_-\), and let \(m_+\) and \(m_-\) be the corresponding densities.  Cauchy--Schwarz and \(\int m_+\,d\nu=\int m_-\,d\nu=1\) give
\[
\begin{aligned}
 \|\mathsf M_+-\mathsf M_-\|_{\mathrm{TV}}
 &=\frac12\int
 |\sqrt{m_+}-\sqrt{m_-}|(\sqrt{m_+}+\sqrt{m_-})\,d\nu\\
 &\leq\frac12 H(\mathsf M_+,\mathsf M_-)
 \left\{\int(\sqrt{m_+}+\sqrt{m_-})^2\,d\nu\right\}^{1/2}\\
 &\leq H(\mathsf M_+,\mathsf M_-)
 \leq\frac12.                                                     \tag{6}
\end{aligned}
\]
Indeed, the second integral in braces is at most \(4\), and the last inequality is (5).

Fix an arbitrary Borel estimator \(T\).  Define a Borel test \(\varphi\in\{+,-\}\) by choosing the value \(\theta_\varepsilon\) nearest to \(T\), with a fixed rule for ties.  If \(\varphi\neq\varepsilon\), the triangle inequality and the definition of the nearest-value test imply
\[
 \Delta
 \leq |T-\theta_+|+|T-\theta_-|
 \leq 2|T-\theta_\varepsilon|,
\]
and hence
\[
 |T-\theta_\varepsilon|\mathbf 1\{\varphi\neq\varepsilon\}
 \geq\frac{\Delta}{2}\mathbf 1\{\varphi\neq\varepsilon\}.     \tag{7}
\]
Support of the priors on the exact model, followed by (7), yields
\[
\begin{aligned}
 &\sup_{P\in\mathcal P(d,\alpha,\beta,\gamma,L)}
 \mathbb E_{P_O^{\otimes n}}|T-\tau_P(x_0)|\\
 &\quad\geq
 \frac12\sum_{\varepsilon\in\{+,-\}}
 \int \mathbb E_{P_O^{\otimes n}}|T-\theta_\varepsilon|\,
 d\Pi_\varepsilon(P)\\
 &\quad\geq
 \frac{\Delta}{4}
 \{\mathsf M_+(\varphi=-)+\mathsf M_-(\varphi=+)\}.             \tag{8}
\end{aligned}
\]
Writing \(B=\{\varphi=+\}\), the variational definition of total variation gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
 \mathsf M_+(\varphi=-)+\mathsf M_-(\varphi=+)
 &=1-\mathsf M_+(B)+\mathsf M_-(B)\\
 &\geq1-\|\mathsf M_+-\mathsf M_-\|_{\mathrm{TV}}
 \geq\frac12,                                                     \tag{9}
\end{aligned}
\]
where the last step is (6).  Combining (3), (8), and (9), and then taking the infimum over all Borel \(T\), proves
\[
 R_n\geq\frac{c_0}{8}\rho_n.                                    \tag{10}
\]
Equations (2) and (10) establish the claimed matched order for every \(n\geq2\).  The constants are uniform in \(x_0\) because both invoked all-point lemmas state that uniformity.  Moreover, (4) consists of mixtures of actual fixed-\(n\) product laws in the exact-uniform, bounded-overlap Bernoulli class, as required.

It remains to classify the oracle boundary.  From \(\mathrm{def:rate}\),
\[
 \eta=\left(1+\frac d{2\gamma}+\frac d{2S}\right)^{-1}.
\]
At \(S=S_\star=d\gamma/(2\gamma+d)\), positivity of \(d\) and \(\gamma\) permits direct substitution:
\[
\begin{aligned}
 \eta
 &=\left(1+\frac d{2\gamma}
          +\frac{2\gamma+d}{2\gamma}\right)^{-1}\\
 &=\frac{\gamma}{2\gamma+d}.                                    \tag{11}
\end{aligned}
\]
Thus \(\rho_n=n^{-\gamma/(2\gamma+d)}\), a pure power with no logarithmic multiplier.  If \(S<S_\star\), then
\[
 \frac d{2S}>1+\frac d{2\gamma},
\]
so, after adding the positive term \(1+d/(2\gamma)\) and taking reciprocals of positive quantities,
\[
 \eta<\left(2+\frac d\gamma\right)^{-1}
 =\frac{\gamma}{2\gamma+d}.                                     \tag{12}
\]
Consequently, (2) and (10) imply
\[
 \frac{R_n}{n^{-\gamma/(2\gamma+d)}}
 \asymp n^{\gamma/(2\gamma+d)-\eta}\longrightarrow\infty.      \tag{13}
\]
Hence the same-class oracle order is attained exactly at the elbow within the stipulated closed regime and is strictly faster than the minimax rate below it.

Finally, if another model contains \(\mathcal P(d,\alpha,\beta,\gamma,L)\) and has the same point target, then for every estimator its supremum risk over the larger model is at least its supremum risk over this subclass.  Taking infima preserves that lower inequality, so (10) transfers to the larger model.  Equation (2) uses the exact-class estimator guarantee and therefore supplies no upper bound for such a larger model.
